import{_ as a,c as t,o as r,ag as n}from"./chunks/framework.Vyheyqg9.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"JavaScript: execution, identity and owned work","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"frontend/javascript.md","filePath":"frontend/javascript.md"}'),s={name:"frontend/javascript.md"};function o(i,e,d,l,c,h){return r(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="javascript-execution-identity-and-owned-work" tabindex="-1">JavaScript: execution, identity and owned work <a class="header-anchor" href="#javascript-execution-identity-and-owned-work" aria-label="Permalink to “JavaScript: execution, identity and owned work”">​</a></h1><p>Prerequisites: native HTML/events and basic functions/objects. Use ES modules for the worked browser code. The language is shared with Node; DOM/event/lifecycle behavior belongs to the browser host.</p><h2 id="reading-order" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">1 </span>Reading order <a class="header-anchor" href="#reading-order" aria-label="Permalink to “1 Reading order”">​</a></h2><ol><li><a href="./../senior_growth/General_ES6/Explain_JavaScript_Code_Execution_177e8106c24d80a0817df62da9bac253.html">Execution, bindings, this and closures</a>, then <a href="./../senior_growth/General_ES6/What_is_the_temporal_dead_zone_Why_is_this_happeni_177e8106c24d80d991b7caa84eaad6b6.html">lexical scope and TDZ</a>.</li><li><a href="./../senior_growth/General_ES6/Can_you_explain_to_me_what_arrow_functions_are_and_177e8106c24d802c83c5d9951499fa84.html">Arrow call-site rules</a>, <a href="./../senior_growth/General_ES6/What_are_pure_impure_functions_177e8106c24d80d19ebde80bcd1b5b50.html">pure transforms</a> and <a href="./../senior_growth/General_ES6/What_is_the_difference_between_&#39;null&#39;_and_&#39;undefin_177e8106c24d80f98ff8c802efb7ff70.html">equality/nullish values</a>.</li><li><a href="./../senior_growth/General_ES6/What_is_a_Promise_177e8106c24d80b3989df334146152f6.html">Promises</a> → <a href="./../senior_growth/General_ES6/Understanding_async_await_in_JavaScript_177e8106c24d806fb457f53520e2c087.html">async/await and total lifetimes</a> → <a href="./../senior_growth/General_ES6/What_are_the_tasks_microtasks_177e8106c24d804f94a8c0f84060cdf2.html">tasks and microtasks</a>.</li><li><a href="./../senior_growth/General_ES6/Could_you_please_describe_with_which_Browser_APIs__177e8106c24d802caa88e9916f01fc53.html">Browser API ownership, events and origins</a>, <a href="./../senior_growth/General_ES6/Explain_history_API_from_Browser_API_177e8106c24d80fcbc17edb92f747483.html">History</a> and optional <a href="./../senior_growth/General_ES6/Browser_Storage_and_Offline_Drafts.html">offline drafts</a>.</li></ol><p>Prototype/class and language-year catalogues remain reference material in <a href="./../senior_growth/General_ES6/">General JavaScript</a>. Use <a href="./../cs-foundations/data-structures.html">CS structures</a> for Map/Set costs and <a href="./../node-js/module-01-nodejs-foundations.html">Node module fundamentals</a> for runtime module boundaries rather than a second copy here.</p><h2 id="values-are-snapshots-references-express-identity" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">2 </span>Values are snapshots; references express identity <a class="header-anchor" href="#values-are-snapshots-references-express-identity" aria-label="Permalink to “2 Values are snapshots; references express identity”">​</a></h2><p>A closure retains lexical bindings, not a frozen copy of every value. An event handler created in one React render sees that render&#39;s bindings; a plain closure over a mutable outer variable can see later assignments. Decide whether an action owns event-time data or deliberately reads current data. Arrow functions capture lexical this; ordinary function this depends on the call site.</p><p>Object spread copies one level. Mutating a nested object through an alias changes every observer of that object. Use explicit immutable updates along changed paths; do not deep-clone every render or compare serialized objects as a substitute for a domain identity. Stable entity IDs, request generations and server versions answer different questions.</p><h2 id="promises-do-not-own-their-underlying-effects" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">3 </span>Promises do not own their underlying effects <a class="header-anchor" href="#promises-do-not-own-their-underlying-effects" aria-label="Permalink to “3 Promises do not own their underlying effects”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Guarantee</th><th>Does not guarantee</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>await</td><td>Resume this async function after settlement</td><td>Main thread moves to a worker</td></tr><tr><td>Promise.all</td><td>Ordered results when all fulfill; rejects on a failure</td><td>Siblings are canceled or drained</td></tr><tr><td>AbortSignal</td><td>Cooperative cancellation request</td><td>Adapter obeys; remote commit is rolled back</td></tr><tr><td>Generation/owner check</td><td>Obsolete result cannot commit locally</td><td>Network or server resources are released</td></tr><tr><td>finally/disposer</td><td>Explicit cleanup path</td><td>Every detached promise is observed automatically</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Give each operation a lifetime: start, deadline, cancellation, result validation, accepted commit and cleanup. Handle expected failures as data when recovery differs; propagate unexpected failures to an owner with safe diagnostics. A try/catch around a timer registration does not catch a later callback rejection. Do not create an unhandled async event callback and assume an Error Boundary will consume it.</p><p>The <a href="./../workbench/frontend/browser/">public-search workbench</a> combines an abort request with a generation guard and disposer. It bounds time and fixed-corpus shape, but a hostile endpoint additionally needs byte/concurrency limits before parsing. The <a href="./../node-js/module-01-nodejs-foundations.html">Node runtime lessons</a> own the larger bounded-I/O mechanisms.</p><h2 id="scheduling-and-resource-cleanup" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">4 </span>Scheduling and resource cleanup <a class="header-anchor" href="#scheduling-and-resource-cleanup" aria-label="Permalink to “4 Scheduling and resource cleanup”">​</a></h2><p>The currently running JavaScript job completes before its queued promise reactions. Microtasks drain at checkpoints and can enqueue more microtasks; repeatedly awaiting an already-resolved promise does not necessarily yield a painted frame. Timers specify eligibility rather than exact execution time. Browser rendering and Node phases are separate host rules.</p><p>Release subscriptions/listeners, timers, observers, sockets and pending readers at the boundary that created them. Remove listeners with the same function identity and capture setting, or use a supported signal-based registration. Disposal should tolerate repetition; invalidate the owner before awaiting cleanup. Garbage collection reclaims unreachable memory eventually, but does not define a timely protocol for external resources.</p><p>Official references checked 2026-10-27: <a href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/multipage/executable-code-and-execution-contexts.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ECMAScript execution contexts</a>, <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#event-loops" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">HTML event loops</a>, <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortController" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">MDN AbortController</a>. Reference host: Node 24.18.0; actual browser observations are in <a href="./verification.html">verification</a>. Apply <a href="./../practice/frontend/f03-javascript.html">F03</a>.</p>',16)])])}const f=a(s,[["render",o]]);export{p as __pageData,f as default};
